{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361399,"nonce":"G636CaS7N46n4eoCAwqzlOkTo6c=","title":"A fiber def cannot perform a resumable effect","message":"`apply_krio_effect_lowering` (crates/zyntax_embed/src/krio_lowering.rs) refuses a `fiber def` that performs an effect with a resumable handler: \"fiber function `NAME` performs a resumable algebraic effect\", continuing that \"the fiber stack switch and the effect state machine have incompatible suspension models\". A resumable perform needs its performer to be a krio state machine that the handler's `k(v)` re-polls, and a fiber body is a stackful function the fiber runtime switches into; the two disagree on any value live across both a perform and a yield. Non-resumable effects inside fibers work: each fiber's handler-stack segment (`__zyntax_effect_fiber_enter`/`_leave`) resolves them against the handlers active at each resume.\n\nWhat exists: the per-fiber handler segment; `krio_fiber_resume_with` and `krio_fiber_take_input` for delivering a value into a suspended fiber; the resumable perform-site protocol in `upgrade_resume_struct_at_perform_sites` (crates/passes/krio_adapter/src/abi_emit.rs).\n\nDone: a fiber body can perform an effect whose handler takes `Resume\u003cT\u003e`. The fiber itself is the continuation: the perform suspends the fiber, the handler runs on the resumer's side, and `k(v)` resumes the fiber with `v`. A second `k` on a fiber continuation is refused, since a fiber stack cannot be copied.","files":null}]}